<p>In the latest development, a U.K.-led virtual meeting of about 40 countries demanded the immediate and unconditional reopening of the Strait of Hormuz, while stopping short of new military commitments for now. Participants said they would intensify diplomatic pressure on Iran and consider coordinated economic and political measures, including sanctions, after Iran’s blockade disrupted a route that normally carries about a fifth of the world’s oil. Several reports said European governments want any larger security role to follow a ceasefire, not come before one, even as President Trump urged allies to take the lead in securing the strait. At the United Nations, the Security Council delayed until Saturday morning, April 4, a vote on a Bahraini draft resolution that would authorize states to use “all defensive means necessary,” or similar language on defensive force, to protect commercial shipping. China publicly opposed any authorization of force, and Russia and France were also reported to object to versions of the text. The diplomacy shows both urgency and a narrow opening: while no broad breakthrough has emerged, reports said Iran has begun negotiating transit for some friendly-nation vessels, and three ships appeared to use a route along Oman’s coast. That suggests limited channels for de-escalation remain, even as the wider Iran war continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7yflbo09m-ItI45Oo2wOF9ppaHahs8qNDJZZHlQWHl2ilKUR3aT3rWdEcQo4XiIchFY456hJsqRpCQLtZfDriPRZ4mBDwfxEDpgAe7XaOwM3' alt='A United Nations Security Council meeting at U.N. headquarters in New York.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>US absent: The United States did not join the U.K.-organized talks, even though the meeting was framed in part around President Trump’s call for other countries to do more on Hormuz.</li><li>Trump pressure: Trump told POLITICO that allies “gotta get guts and go in,” sharpening the contrast between Washington’s push for maritime action and Europe’s wait-for-ceasefire position.</li><li>GCC push: Gulf Cooperation Council Secretary General Jassem al-Budaiwi said Iran had not only blocked tankers but also imposed conditions on some vessels seeking passage, and he urged the Security Council to take all necessary measures to protect maritime routes.</li><li>Threat exchange: As diplomacy continued, President Trump warned of additional U.S. strikes on Iranian infrastructure, while Iranian officials warned against what they called a provocative U.N. vote, underscoring the narrow room for compromise.</li><li>Who was in the room: The 41-nation call included representatives from the European Union, France, Germany, Bahrain, the United Arab Emirates, and Canada, showing how the response brought together European, Gulf, and transatlantic partners.</li></ul><blockquote>Iran is trying to hold the global economy hostage in the Strait of Hormuz. They must not prevail. - Yvette Cooper</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United Kingdom: Yvette Cooper said the shipping disruption was harming countries that “played no part in this conflict,” framing the push to reopen the strait as protection for uninvolved trading states as much as a response to Iran. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/02/coalition-countries-ships-strait-of-hormuz-yvette-cooper'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Oman: Iran said it was drafting a protocol with Oman to oversee transit through Hormuz, pointing to Muscat’s potential role as a coastal intermediary rather than a backer of force. (<a href='https://www.aa.com.tr/en/middle-east/iran-says-drafting-protocol-with-oman-to-oversee-strait-of-hormuz-transit/3889814'>Anadolu Agency</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/europe-up-policing-hormuz-strait-only-fighting-stops/'>Europe up for policing Hormuz — but only if the fighting stops</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/un-vote-hormuz-resolution-china-opposes-authorization-force'>UN to vote on Hormuz resolution as China opposes authorization of force</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/plus/economy/article/3348877/strait-hormuz-crisis'>Strait of Hormuz crisis</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/02/coalition-countries-ships-strait-of-hormuz-yvette-cooper'>Coalition of countries discuss ‘every possible measure’ to pressure Iran into reopening strait of Hormuz</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987881/gulf-states-seek-un-mandate-for-force-to-protect-strait-of-hormuz'>Gulf states seek UN mandate for force to protect Strait of Hormuz</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/world-anxious-to-open-hormuz-strait-while-trump-and-iran-trade-threats2'>World anxious to open Hormuz Strait while Trump and Iran trade threats</a> - japantoday.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-unsc-to-vote-on-gulf-led-resolution-to-open-hormuz/live-76649854'>Iran war: UNSC to vote on Gulf-led resolution to open Hormuz</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/04/02/kmgr-a02.html'>Britain hosts Strait of Hormuz crisis summit amid breakdown of relations with US</a> - wsws.org</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-strait-of-hormuz-iran-control/'>Trump sends mixed messages on securing the Strait of Hormuz</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/global-leaders-work-to-ease-oil-price-surge-as-trump-signals-more-weeks-of-iran-war'>Global leaders work to ease oil price surge as Trump signals more weeks of Iran war</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/02/world/uk-reopen-strait-of-hormuz-talks.html'>U.K. to Host Talks on Securing Strait of Hormuz</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/02/nx-s1-5771446/u-k-convenes-40-nations-to-discuss-strait-of-hormuz'>U.K. convenes 40 nations to discuss Strait of Hormuz</a> - npr.org</li><